    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5440",
      "problem": "Given the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14152450/assets/07a0136c99e832ac8839b93b75c5dcdbb4c7f3298c0ee41546389649ac17a3ea.svg) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14152450/assets/05f4b52e6db509788a7f98278fa003da71fde06817e700d7bbb9fe4827e9551a.svg)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$",
